          ><div class="iysyaZoxaZfzSXIL2" data-instant-type="container"
            ><div data-instant-type="text" class="instant-rich-text ixthxZqJQMFIS14hG"
              ><h3><strong>Our Most Primitive Calming Mechanism</strong></h3></div
            ><div data-instant-type="text" class="instant-rich-text i6qBQ2V4C4Flm8lEG"
              ><p>Have you ever wondered why, when we’re stressed, we instinctively bring our hands to our faces?</p><p></p><p>It’s not a coincidence. The <strong>"Hand-to-Mouth" gesture</strong> is the most deeply ingrained calming reflex in the human nervous system. </p><p></p><p>From the moment we are born, this specific motion is our first and most powerful way to self-soothe. It’s hardwired into our DNA to signal "safety" to the brain.</p><p></p><p>When the world gets too loud, your nervous system searches for that "pause" button. It’s looking for the physical comfort of the ritual.</p></div
            ></div
          ></div
        ></div
